WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:05.279
For years, the top of the billionaires list
has been dominated by various tech moguls

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:10.030
from Bill Gates and Mark Zuckerberg to Jeff
Bezos and Elon Musk.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:16.700
On May 24, 2021 though, Bernard Arnault, the
CEO of LVMH dethroned all the tech moguls

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.720
and became the richest person in the world
with a net worth over $190 billion.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.730
So, here’s how a fashion tycoon was able
to beat out the owners of the largest tech

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.600
companies in the world.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.280
Taking a look back at Bernard’s roots, he
was born on March 5, 1949 in Roubaix, France.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.620
Unlike his peers at the top, Bernard was not
only born into a well off family, but an opulent

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.330
family who ran a well respected construction
business within France.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:48.610
Bernard never really had that much interest
in the construction business; Bernard’s

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.940
true passion was always music.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.900
But he was also practical and knew that it
would be nearly impossible to make it as a

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.340
concert pianist.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.460
Then again, he did eventually become the richest
person in the world, so maybe he would’ve

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.000
made it as a concert pianist as well.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.300
Anyway, Bernard chose to go the safe route,
deciding to get an engineering degree instead.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.800
Bernard would attend one of the most prestigious
universities in France: École Polytechnique,

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.690
and he would get a job at his father’s construction
company in 1971.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:23.970
That same year, Bernard visited the US and
this would accidentally turn into a pivotal

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.950
point in his career.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.430
While in New York, Bernard would ride in a
taxi, and in an attempt to start up some small

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:35.220
talk, Bernard would ask the taxi driver if
he knew the president of France.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.510
The taxi driver replied that he didn’t know
the president of France but he did know Christion

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:41.510
Dior.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:46.710
Ironically, 50 years later, the average American
still doesn’t know the president of France,

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.049
but we all know about Christian Dior.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:53.340
This exchange showed Bernard the international
recognition of French fashion design, and

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.500
it made him extremely proud to be French.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.880
For a moment, he dreamed about running a French
fashion company that was known around the

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.100
world.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.760
But once again, Bernard was extremely practical,
and he knew that starting his own fashion

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.540
brand would be nearly impossible.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.430
So, he simply got back to work with his father
after returning to France.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.680
Over the next couple of years, Bernard would
convince his father to transition the company

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.799
from being a construction company to being
a real estate company.

00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:23.749
And as the real estate company grew, Bernard
would become the company’s president in

00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:26.450
1978 at age 29.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:31.779
After taking lead, Bernard would attempt to
expand the company to America in 1981, but

00:02:31.779 --> 00:02:33.999
these efforts wouldn’t last that long.

00:02:33.999 --> 00:02:38.909
You see, in 1984, the parent company of Christian
Dior, Boussac, went bankrupt.

00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:42.299
And the French government was looking for
a buyer.

00:02:42.299 --> 00:02:46.409
At the time, Bernard was quite wealthy, but
he had nowhere near enough money to buy a

00:02:46.409 --> 00:02:48.730
large brand like Christian Dior.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.749
He knew that this may be his only chance to
buy the company though, so he started talking

00:02:52.749 --> 00:02:54.079
to some bankers.

00:02:54.079 --> 00:02:59.090
Bernard would eventually land on Antoine Bernheim
from Lazard financial services who agreed

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:02.469
to buy up the rest of Boussac and let Bernard
run the company.

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:07.900
Bernard would end up buying Boussac for $80
million out of which he supplied $15 million

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.379
and Lazard supplied $65 million.

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:13.449
As soon as the acquisition went through, Bernard
got to work.

00:03:13.449 --> 00:03:18.150
And as we previously discussed, Bernard was
extremely practical, and this required him

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.930
to make ruthless decisions.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.579
Bernard didn’t really care about Boussac,
the only reason he bought the company was

00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:25.339
to acquire Christion Dior.

00:03:25.339 --> 00:03:29.879
So, his first order of business was to revive
the other companies owned by Boussac and then

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:30.980
sell them.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:36.099
He started off by firing 9000 employees and
dramatically leaning up the various businesses.

00:03:36.099 --> 00:03:40.579
He would then flip these businesses for a
total of $500 million.

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:45.419
This was extremely uncharacteristic of French
businessmen at the time who generally prioritized

00:03:45.419 --> 00:03:47.650
empathy over profits.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:50.230
This wasn’t necessarily because they were
super nice individuals.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:55.230
They did, however, care a great deal about
being viewed as empathetic businessmen.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.199
Bernard didn’t really care about creating
such a perception though, and he just ate

00:03:59.199 --> 00:04:02.069
up all the criticism he received from the
media.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:06.370
One of the most iconic names given to Bernard
by the media was “the wolf in the cashmere

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:07.400
coat.”

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.959
Bernard would just continue on his path though,
and his next goal was to complete his Christian

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:13.419
Dior acquisition.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:17.480
He already bought most of the brand when he
bought Boussac, but the perfume division of

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.950
Christian Dior had been previously sold to
Louis Vuitton Moet Hennessy.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.590
So, Bernard still had to buy back the perfume
division to complete his acquisition.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:30.010
In order to accomplish this, Bernard would
befriend the leader of the Louis Vuitton Brand.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:34.420
At the time, the leaders of the two brands
Louis Vuitton and Moet Hennessy were having

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.830
a major fall out.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:39.530
Bernard would side with the Louis Vuitton
boss to oust the leader of Moet Hennessy and

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:40.970
get himself into the company.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:46.390
Eventually, he would turn on the Louis Vuitton
boss as well, making himself the leader of

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:47.620
LVMH.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.830
He didn’t have any equity in the business
though, so building up equity would be his

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.010
next target.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:55.920
Bernard would pour in all the money he made
from flipping Boussac’s businesses, and

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.130
he would garner additional funding from Lazard
to buy a significant stake in LVMH.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.870
By 1990, Bernard was able to take control
of LVMH.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.370
Let’s just keep in mind, 6 years before
this, he was trying to expand a family real

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.090
estate company to America.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:15.820
So, between 1984 and 1990, Bernard went from
a generic real estate businessman worth tens

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:21.040
of millions of dollars to being the CEO of
LVMH worth hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.720
That’s really quite a transition, but Bernard
was just getting started on his luxury French

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.070
empire.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:31.620
Over the next few decades, Bernard simply
went on a buying spree picking up any and

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.260
every fashion company he could get his hands
on.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.000
Some of his most notable purchases include
Celine in 1996, Sephora in 1997, Bulgari in

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.330
2011, and Tiffany &amp; Co just earlier this year.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:49.790
Today, LVMH owns an impressive 75 different
brands which are divided into 5 major sectors.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:54.390
The first sector is Fashion &amp; Leather which
is dominated by Louis Vuitton.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:58.480
The second sector is perfume and cosmetics
and this sector is dominated by Christian

00:05:58.480 --> 00:05:59.610
Dior.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:04.490
The third sector is Watches &amp; Jewelry and
this sector is dominated by Bulgari.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:09.750
The fourth sector is selective retailing &amp; other,
and this sector is led by Sephora.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:14.750
And finally, the last sector is wine &amp; spirits
which is dominated by Hennessey.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:19.310
Despite this meteoric success, Bernard Arnault
has had his share of major failures as well

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:23.950
such as his attempt to acquire Gucci in 1999
which is known as one of the most bitter fights

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.030
in corporate history.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.420
In 1999, Bernard decided that he wanted to
buy Gucci.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:33.250
Gucci valued their autonomy though, and they
weren’t looking to be acquired.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:38.130
So, Bernard would just go onto the open stock
market and start buying shares of Gucci.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.650
He started off small, buying just 5% of the
company.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:46.680
The CEO of Gucci at the time, Domenico De
Sole, was promptly notified of the purchase

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.810
and De Sole instantly knew what this meant.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:49.810
Arnault promised that the purchase was just
a small passive investment, but De Sole saw

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:50.810
right through this facade.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.900
The Gucci board would immediately organize
a meeting, and they would discuss the two

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:54.570
ways this could play out.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.610
Either Bernard would make an offer to buy
the entire company or he would continue to

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:02.260
buy shares from the open market until he could
force an acquisition.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:05.900
Bernard chose to go with the second option
and he would quickly accumulate a stake in

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.260
Gucci through the open market and a private
deal.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:13.800
In just a couple of weeks, LVMH would up their
stake to 26.7%.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.770
Soon after Bernard would have a meeting with
De Sole during which Bernard would ask for

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.400
3 seats on the board.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.060
De Sole would reject this offer, and Bernard
would just continue to buy up more shares

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:26.530
of Gucci, upping his stake to 34.4%.

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:30.980
This move was on purpose and was designed
to put massive pressure onto Gucci.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:35.920
You see, the creative director at Gucci, Tom
Ford, had a contract that allowed him to leave

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.880
the company with no penalties if any one individual
were to garner a 35% stake in the company.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:46.270
Having no choice, De Sole would offer Bernard
two seats on the board, but Bernard would

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:47.670
reject this offer.

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:51.440
Bernard would go on to call a special meeting
with the board members which he could do because

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.650
of his large stake in the company.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.770
Knowing what this would lead to, De Sole would
simply give in and offer LVMH the opportunity

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.150
to buy the entire company for $85 per share.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:05.040
Bernard could have picked up the company right
then and there, but Bernard would decide to

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.900
show some mercy and he wouldn’t take on
the offer.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:12.180
Meanwhile, De Sole would come up with a last
resort idea that would end up saving Gucci

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:13.550
from LVMH.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.790
De Sole would create an Employee Stock Ownership
Plan.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:21.090
This plan entailed diluting all the shareholders
of Gucci and creating stock that would eventually

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.940
be awarded to employees.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.690
The creation of the employee stock ownership
plan increased the total amount of Gucci stock

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:33.729
available by 42%, and this subsequently reduced
LVMH’s ownership of Gucci from 34.4% down

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:35.110
to 20%.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:40.110
Bernard was obviously furious, but he didn’t
think he was going to lose Gucci.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.110
He simply thought that Gucci wanted LVMH to
pay a premium to buy the company.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:48.589
So, Bernard would start negotiations with
Gucci as to who would purchase the newly created

00:08:48.589 --> 00:08:49.589
stock.

00:08:49.589 --> 00:08:53.180
But little did Bernard know that De Sole was
actually talking with Francois Pinault who

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.550
is the leader of the Kering Group.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:09:00.459
And De Sole would end up selling the 42% stake
to the Kering Group for $3 billion.

00:09:00.459 --> 00:09:05.470
After seeing this, Bernard would become livid
and he would sue to block the deal.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.160
LVMH would offer $85 per share to buy the
entire company and LVMH would argue through

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:13.089
the courts that Gucci must consider their
offer.

00:09:13.089 --> 00:09:17.610
The courts would agree that Gucci must consider
Bernard’s offer, but that Gucci didn’t

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.529
need to take their offer.

00:09:19.529 --> 00:09:23.600
Gucci would end up rejecting Bernard’s offer
and they would choose to sell the 42% stake

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.339
to the Kering group for $75 per share.

00:09:26.339 --> 00:09:31.959
The Kering group would go on to buy the rest
from LVMH at $94 per share in October of 2001.

00:09:31.959 --> 00:09:34.639
Both sides would end up calling the outcome
a victory.

00:09:34.639 --> 00:09:40.550
Gucci was able to avoid being acquired by
LVMH, and LVMH was able to profit $700 million

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.910
by flipping the shares to the Kering group.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:48.480
But in reality, I think Gucci unfortunately
lost this battle because they ended up diluting

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.470
their shares and still getting acquired anyway.

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:59.570
Throughout the 2000s, Bernard would attempt
a similar strategy with Hermes, but he would

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:01.970
eventually end up backing away from that acquisition
as well.

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.240
At the end of the day, though Bernard did
have some sour deals, most of his acquisitions

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.779
have gone on without a hitch, and this has
allowed him to build LVMH to be the largest

00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:15.149
fashion company in the world and the 18th
largest company in the world with a market

00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:17.899
cap of about $400 billion.

00:10:17.899 --> 00:10:24.920
With a 46.84% stake in LVMH, Bernard has successfully
built up a fortune worth just under $200 billion

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:25.920
today.

